        heading: "Roads, Drainage, and County Standards",
        body: "Paving a colonia's roads is pavement engineering on difficult ground. The existing dirt roads are proof-rolled and tested, unsuitable material is removed, and the new section is built up with compacted base and an asphalt or concrete surface designed for the axle loads of buses, fire trucks, and delivery vehicles. In expansive clay areas, the design includes moisture control, lime or cement stabilization of the subgrade where the geotechnical report recommends it, and drainage that keeps the subgrade from cycling wet and dry. The road profile is set to drain, with cross-slope and ditches that move stormwater to an outfall instead of letting it pond on the new pavement.\n\nDrainage in the flat borderlands is a grading exercise. With little natural fall, the engineer designs shallow ditches, culverts at every crossing, and detention or outfall channels that carry the design storm without flooding homes. Lot grading keeps finished floors above the surrounding grade, and the drainage plan is reviewed against the county's standards and, where floodplain is involved, the county floodplain administrator's requirements. Because the county owns and maintains the public roads and drainage, the design follows the county's construction standards and inspection process, and the project closes out with as-built records the county can maintain for decades.",
